Bug: after a restart the scan adds its includes to CUs that already have knowledge

The scan records an include reference at line 1, column 0 of the includer for every
#include "..." line it finds, whether an #ifdef takes it or not. Within a session that
does no harm: parsing a CU removes the references in it first. But a new session loads
the snapshot and then runs the scan again over every CU, and a CU that is current is not
parsed, so the scan's references stay beside the parse's. Three effects:

- the table after a restart differs from the one before, for the same sources,
- a.c is recorded as including x.h, although its #ifdef does not take it,
- b.c is recorded as including x.h twice, on line 1 and on line 3. Browsing the include
  lists line 1 of b.c as an includer.

Likely fix: the scan records include references only for a CU without knowledge, since
a parse has already recorded better ones. Not checked: Rename Module and Move Function,
which find #include lines through these references.

Backlog item include-edges in doc/backlog.md. It does not block out-of-date, whose
include closure only has to be a superset.
